1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,182
You mount a disk by hand, everything works, and then you reboot. The mount is gone.

2
00:00:05,202 --> 00:00:07,901
To make it permanent, you write it into a file.

3
00:00:07,921 --> 00:00:10,620
And how you name the disk in that file matters.

4
00:00:11,090 --> 00:00:15,230
In this video: mount file systems at boot, by UUID or label.

5
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:22,333
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:22,803 --> 00:00:28,607
Configure systems to mount file systems at boot by universally unique ID, or label.

7
00:00:29,077 --> 00:00:32,535
We'll find the label and the UUID of two volumes,

8
00:00:32,555 --> 00:00:37,291
mount one by label and one by UUID, and prove it survives a reboot.

9
00:00:46,811 --> 00:00:52,612
Linux reads the file slash etc slash fstab at boot to know what to mount, and where.

10
00:00:52,632 --> 00:00:56,562
Each line names a disk, a folder, and a file system type.

11
00:01:04,032 --> 00:01:08,991
You could name the disk by its device name, like slash dev slash vdb.

12
00:01:09,011 --> 00:01:12,888
But device names can change from one boot to the next.

13
00:01:12,908 --> 00:01:18,156
A label or a UUID belongs to the file system itself, so it never changes.

14
00:01:26,626 --> 00:01:29,912
A label is a short name that a person chooses.

15
00:01:29,932 --> 00:01:34,870
A UUID is a long unique number, made when the file system is created.

16
00:01:34,890 --> 00:01:36,739
Both are fine on the exam.

17
00:01:44,209 --> 00:01:49,524
The lab already created two logical volumes and put an ext4 file system on each.

18
00:01:49,544 --> 00:01:53,192
You will learn to create file systems in a later video.

19
00:02:00,712 --> 00:02:06,298
First, findmnt and a folder. It prints nothing: nothing is mounted there.

20
00:02:06,318 --> 00:02:09,678
Then blkid, with sudo, and the first volume.

21
00:02:09,698 --> 00:02:14,439
It prints the device, its LABEL, its UUID, and its TYPE, ext4.

22
00:02:14,459 --> 00:02:16,820
This one has the label appdata.

23
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:30,030
The second volume has a UUID, but no label. So we will mount it by UUID.

24
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:40,168
First the folder where it will be mounted, with mkdir.

25
00:02:40,188 --> 00:02:42,796
Then we add one line to the fstab file.

26
00:02:42,816 --> 00:02:46,838
Echo prints the line, and tee dash a appends it to the file.

27
00:02:46,858 --> 00:02:49,735
The pipe sends the output of echo into tee.

28
00:02:56,205 --> 00:03:01,825
The line says: this label, this folder, ext4, default options, and two zeros.

29
00:03:01,845 --> 00:03:06,074
Then daemon-reload tells systemd to read the changed file.

30
00:03:06,094 --> 00:03:11,055
And mount dash a mounts everything in fstab that is not mounted yet.

31
00:03:18,525 --> 00:03:22,532
findmnt now shows the folder, mounted from the logical volume.

32
00:03:22,552 --> 00:03:25,779
If mount dash a prints no error, the line is good.

33
00:03:33,299 --> 00:03:36,828
Now the same with the UUID. The folder first.

34
00:03:36,848 --> 00:03:43,058
Then the line starts with UUID, an equals sign, and the long number from blkid.

35
00:03:43,078 --> 00:03:45,503
Same folder, type, and options.

36
00:03:51,973 --> 00:03:57,670
Again daemon-reload and mount dash a. And findmnt shows the second volume mounted.

37
00:04:05,190 --> 00:04:08,460
Here are our two lines in fstab. Six fields:

38
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:14,143
the device, by LABEL or UUID, the folder, the file system type, the options,

39
00:04:14,163 --> 00:04:19,975
and two numbers for old backup and file system check tools. Zero zero is fine.

40
00:04:33,495 --> 00:04:37,733
A warning. A wrong line in fstab can stop the system from booting.

41
00:04:37,753 --> 00:04:41,280
So never reboot before testing: run mount dash a first.

42
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:44,312
If it prints an error, fix the line right away.

43
00:04:53,832 --> 00:04:57,268
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

44
00:04:58,788 --> 00:05:04,645
We reboot. Then findmnt shows both folders mounted again, by label and by UUID,

45
00:05:04,665 --> 00:05:07,472
without anyone typing a mount command.

46
00:05:16,992 --> 00:05:20,922
Your turn. A volume with the label examlabel is ready.

47
00:05:20,942 --> 00:05:27,066
Create the folder slash mnt slash exam. Add a line to fstab that mounts it by label.

48
00:05:27,086 --> 00:05:29,260
Mount it, and run the checker.

49
00:05:35,730 --> 00:05:37,481
Try before you open the solution.

50
00:05:43,001 --> 00:05:47,738
Next up: add partitions, logical volumes and swap non-destructively.

51
00:05:48,208 --> 00:05:51,111
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
